N:HOW READ (2026-11-11): No source plate exists because this is a new composition; this is the compositional audit. Every bar totals four eighths (2/4). Bar 1 D4 D4 quarters; bar 2 D4 quarter, quarter rest; bar 3 F#4 F#4 quarters; bar 4 F#4 F#4 eighths, quarter rest; bar 5 D4 E4 eighths, F#4 quarter; bar 6 a two-beat rest; bar 7 F#4 E4 eighths, D4 quarter; bar 8 D4 quarter, quarter rest. Pitch set D-E-F#, range D4-F#4, largest interval a major third (D4-F#4, bars 2-3 and 4-5). The earlier draft's C4-G4 fifth (analyzer level 5) is gone: the analyzer now reads level 2, its hardest dimension being the rests. Its Ontario line reads Grade 5 only because it counts the two-sharp key signature and the chord symbol, which JK children never read; they learn the song by rote and only beat, sound quality and volume are claimed. Originality: the do-re-mi calls on repeated notes are common property; the falling mi-re-do cadence (bar 7) is the same three notes as "Hot Cross Buns" but in a different rhythm and context; checked against "Bounce High, Bounce Low", "Rain, Rain", "Hot Cross Buns", "Mary Had a Little Lamb" and "Frère Jacques" with no shared opening. Kodo's O-daiko is unpitched ensemble drumming; nothing of it is taken.
N:UNCERTAIN: Whether JK children can sing the BOOM low and the taps a third higher, whether one silent bar at quarter = 84 (about 1.4 seconds) is long enough for a chosen body sound, whether the stomp is safe and inclusive for every child, and whether a 16-second clip is enough listening. A classroom trial, a taiko-informed educator, an accessibility reviewer and a second score reader must settle these.
